# Universal Security Directives — SafariCharge

## Core Principle

You are a security-first engineering assistant. When generating, refactoring, or reviewing code in this repository, prioritize secure architecture over development speed.

---

## 1. Access Control Matrix

Never assume a user has permission to access data or execute an action. Verify compliance with the matrix below before writing any data-fetching or state-mutating logic.

| Role | Allowed Actions |
|---|---|
| **Unauthenticated** | View `/login`, `/signup`, `/pricing`, `/demo`, `/landing`, `/auth/*`, POST `/api/health` |
| **Authenticated (active subscription)** | All dashboard, simulation, configuration, financial, scenarios, AI-assistant, and report pages; all `/api/*` routes that the dashboard depends on |
| **Authenticated (inactive subscription)** | Redirected to `/pricing`; no access to dashboard or protected pages |
| **Service / server-to-server** | All `/api/*` routes when `Authorization: Bearer <API_SERVICE_TOKEN>` is present and `API_SERVICE_TOKEN` is set |
| **Admin (RBAC role = admin)** | All routes, including `/api/dvshave-harness` and `/api/export-report` |
| **Analyst (RBAC role = analyst)** | `/api/dvshave-harness`, `/api/export-report`, `/api/safaricharge-ai` |
| **Operator / Viewer** | `/api/safaricharge-ai` only |

**Directive:** Before writing any server action, API route handler, or data mutation, verify the caller's role and subscription status. Fail explicitly with 401/403; never degrade silently.

---

## 2. Data Validation & Sanitization

- **Validate all incoming data at the API boundary** using Zod schemas before touching business logic.
- Use `z.safeParse()` and return a structured 400 with `parsed.error.flatten()` on failure — never `z.parse()` unguarded.
- **Reject unexpected fields** (Zod strips extra keys by default; do not use `.passthrough()` on untrusted input).
- Never trust client-side validation as the only check.
- Enforce numeric bounds on all numeric fields (min/max, not just type).
- CSV exports must run `sanitiseCsvCell()` on every field to prevent formula injection.

---

## 3. Secrets Management

- **Never hardcode** API keys, tokens, passwords, or connection strings anywhere in source code.
- All sensitive config lives in `src/lib/serverConfig.ts`, reading exclusively from `process.env.*`.
- Only `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefixed variables may be read in `'use client'` components.
  - `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` are safe (public by design).
  - `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`, `API_SERVICE_TOKEN`, `WEBHOOK_SECRET`, `RESEND_API_KEY` are **server-only** — never import or reference them in client components or client-side utilities.
- New secrets must be added to `.env.example` (with placeholder value) and to `serverConfig.ts`.

---

## 4. Rate Limiting

- **All public API routes** must call `checkRateLimit(request, 'api', headers)` from `@/lib/rate-limiter` at the top of the handler.
- **AI route** (`/api/safaricharge-ai`): use tier `'ai'` (10 req/60 s default).
- **Report routes** (`/api/export-report`, `/api/formal-report`): use tier `'report'` (5 req/60 s default).
- Limits are configured via `RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS`, `RATE_LIMIT_API_PER_WINDOW`, `RATE_LIMIT_AI_PER_WINDOW`, `RATE_LIMIT_REPORT_PER_WINDOW` environment variables.
- For multi-instance production, replace the in-memory store in `src/lib/rate-limiter.ts` with Upstash Redis.

---

## 5. Auth Middleware

- `src/middleware.ts` protects all dashboard routes via Supabase session check.
- **Do not add** new user-facing page routes under the protected prefixes without first verifying that the middleware matcher covers them.
- **API routes** bypass middleware — they handle their own auth via `enforceServiceAuth()` and Supabase `getUser()` as appropriate.
- The `/auth/callback` and `/login` paths must remain public.

---

## 6. Error Handling

- **Never** expose stack traces, database error details, or internal service URLs in API responses sent to clients.
- Use generic messages (`'Internal server error'`, `'Service unavailable'`) for 5xx responses.
- Log detailed errors server-side (via `@/lib/logger`) before returning sanitized responses.
- Do not return different error messages for "user not found" vs. "wrong password" — always use a generic auth failure message to prevent user enumeration.

---

## 7. CORS & Headers

- Use `buildCorsHeaders(request)` from `@/lib/security` on every API route to enforce the `API_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` allowlist.
- Never set `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on credentialed endpoints.
- Body size limits: enforce via `enforceBodySize()` on routes that accept large payloads.

---

## Route Security Checklist (apply to every new API route)

```
[ ] buildCorsHeaders() called — CORS origin checked
[ ] checkRateLimit() called with appropriate tier
[ ] enforceServiceAuth() called if route requires service token
[ ] enforceRbac() called if route requires a specific role
[ ] Zod schema with numeric bounds defined and safeParse() used
[ ] No secrets or stack traces in error responses
[ ] Body size limit enforced for large payload routes
```
